1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,790
Two batsmen play ten matches each.

2
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:05,494
Both score five hundred and thirty runs.

3
00:00:05,494 --> 00:00:07,256
Both average fifty three.

4
00:00:07,656 --> 00:00:11,617
But batsman A swings from zero
to one hundred and seventeen.

5
00:00:11,617 --> 00:00:14,585
Batsman B stays between
forty six and sixty.

6
00:00:15,035 --> 00:00:18,838
Aarav found these scores in our NCERT
book, and asked a simple question.

7
00:00:19,188 --> 00:00:21,193
Which batsman is more consistent?

8
00:00:21,193 --> 00:00:23,025
And can one number prove it?

9
00:00:23,525 --> 00:00:25,988
That number is the standard deviation.

10
00:00:25,988 --> 00:00:28,715
To find it, we first meet the variance.

11
00:00:29,365 --> 00:00:30,402
Quick reminder.

12
00:00:30,402 --> 00:00:34,727
Last time, we measured spread with
distances from the mean, using absolute

13
00:00:34,727 --> 00:00:35,627
values.

14
00:00:35,738 --> 00:00:39,986
Today there is another way to stop
the minus signs from cancelling.

15
00:00:39,986 --> 00:00:41,539
Square every deviation.

16
00:00:41,539 --> 00:00:43,408
A square is never negative.

17
00:00:44,058 --> 00:00:48,819
Watch it on eight small numbers: two,
four, four, four, five, five, seven

18
00:00:48,819 --> 00:00:49,719
and nine.

19
00:00:49,573 --> 00:00:50,897
Their mean is five.

20
00:00:51,297 --> 00:00:54,263
Each gap from five becomes
the side of a real square.

21
00:00:54,263 --> 00:00:56,504
A gap of three makes a square of nine.

22
00:00:56,504 --> 00:00:58,829
A gap of four makes a square of sixteen.

23
00:00:59,229 --> 00:01:01,231
Now add all the square areas.

24
00:01:01,231 --> 00:01:05,013
Nine, one, one, one, zero,
zero, four and sixteen.

25
00:01:05,013 --> 00:01:06,715
The total is thirty two.

26
00:01:07,115 --> 00:01:09,172
Share it among the eight numbers.

27
00:01:09,172 --> 00:01:11,094
Thirty two over eight is four.

28
00:01:11,094 --> 00:01:13,661
That is the variance: the average square.

29
00:01:14,061 --> 00:01:16,603
Now take the side of that average square.

30
00:01:16,603 --> 00:01:18,633
The square root of four is two.

31
00:01:18,633 --> 00:01:20,553
That is the standard deviation.

32
00:01:21,203 --> 00:01:23,002
Now the rule, step by step.

33
00:01:23,402 --> 00:01:24,302
Step one.

34
00:01:24,280 --> 00:01:26,101
Find the mean, x bar.

35
00:01:26,851 --> 00:01:27,751
Step two.

36
00:01:27,724 --> 00:01:31,507
Find each deviation, x
minus x bar, and square it.

37
00:01:32,257 --> 00:01:33,157
Step three.

38
00:01:33,155 --> 00:01:35,517
Add the squares, and divide by n.

39
00:01:35,517 --> 00:01:38,416
This is the variance,
written sigma squared.

40
00:01:39,166 --> 00:01:40,077
Step four.

41
00:01:40,077 --> 00:01:42,224
Take the positive square root.

42
00:01:42,224 --> 00:01:44,957
This is the standard deviation, sigma.

43
00:01:45,707 --> 00:01:46,916
A useful shortcut.

44
00:01:46,916 --> 00:01:51,459
The variance is also the mean of the
squares, minus the square of the mean.

45
00:01:52,109 --> 00:01:53,163
Side by side.

46
00:01:53,163 --> 00:01:56,980
The variance squares the gaps,
so its unit is squared too.

47
00:01:56,980 --> 00:02:00,845
For runs, that is runs squared,
which is hard to picture.

48
00:02:01,245 --> 00:02:05,720
The standard deviation takes the
square root, so it is back in runs.

49
00:02:05,720 --> 00:02:07,853
Batsman A has about thirty six.

50
00:02:07,853 --> 00:02:10,244
Batsman B has about four point two.

51
00:02:10,844 --> 00:02:11,744
Your turn.

52
00:02:11,661 --> 00:02:13,729
Which batsman is more consistent?

53
00:02:13,729 --> 00:02:14,885
Pause and think.

54
00:02:18,085 --> 00:02:18,985
Batsman B!

55
00:02:18,954 --> 00:02:23,370
A smaller standard deviation means
the scores stay close to the mean.

56
00:02:23,370 --> 00:02:25,419
Batsman A's scores jump around.

57
00:02:26,069 --> 00:02:27,037
Remember this.

58
00:02:27,037 --> 00:02:30,566
Square the gaps, average them,
then take the square root.

59
00:02:31,266 --> 00:02:32,956
Now, one worked example.

60
00:02:33,306 --> 00:02:37,826
Find the variance and the standard
deviation of six, eight, ten, and so

61
00:02:37,826 --> 00:02:39,517
on, up to twenty four.

62
00:02:40,017 --> 00:02:40,917
Step one.

63
00:02:40,796 --> 00:02:43,874
There are ten numbers,
and the mean is fifteen.

64
00:02:44,624 --> 00:02:45,524
Step two.

65
00:02:45,339 --> 00:02:47,852
The deviations run from
minus nine to nine.

66
00:02:47,852 --> 00:02:52,154
Their squares are eighty one, forty
nine, twenty five, nine and one, twice

67
00:02:52,154 --> 00:02:53,054
each.

68
00:02:53,393 --> 00:02:54,293
Step three.

69
00:02:54,140 --> 00:02:56,688
The squares add up to
three hundred and thirty.

70
00:02:57,438 --> 00:02:58,338
Step four.

71
00:02:58,294 --> 00:02:59,404
Divide by ten.

72
00:02:59,404 --> 00:03:01,370
The variance is thirty three.

73
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:03,020
Step five.

74
00:03:02,971 --> 00:03:07,545
The standard deviation is the square
root of thirty three, about five point

75
00:03:07,545 --> 00:03:08,445
seven four.

76
00:03:09,095 --> 00:03:10,556
Now, the exam trap.

77
00:03:10,906 --> 00:03:14,780
Many students stop at the variance,
and write the standard deviation as

78
00:03:14,780 --> 00:03:15,680
thirty three.

79
00:03:16,075 --> 00:03:20,008
But the standard deviation is
the square root of the variance.

80
00:03:20,008 --> 00:03:23,794
Here it is about five point
seven four, not thirty three.

81
00:03:24,444 --> 00:03:25,734
Where is this asked?

82
00:03:25,734 --> 00:03:26,634
In JEE Main.

83
00:03:26,626 --> 00:03:30,461
In February twenty twenty three, a
paper gave the mean and variance of

84
00:03:30,461 --> 00:03:33,656
five observations, and asked
about the two missing ones.

85
00:03:34,256 --> 00:03:35,428
Exam level now.

86
00:03:35,428 --> 00:03:38,019
A sixth observation, four, is added.

87
00:03:38,019 --> 00:03:39,598
Find the new variance.

88
00:03:39,598 --> 00:03:40,820
Pause and solve.

89
00:03:46,220 --> 00:03:47,702
The answer is B.

90
00:03:47,702 --> 00:03:49,115
Five over three.

91
00:03:49,615 --> 00:03:51,448
Use the shortcut backwards.

92
00:03:51,448 --> 00:03:54,527
The sum is five times
four, which is twenty.

93
00:03:54,527 --> 00:03:59,139
The sum of squares is five times,
two plus sixteen, which is ninety.

94
00:03:59,639 --> 00:04:00,644
Add the four.

95
00:04:00,644 --> 00:04:04,969
Now the sum is twenty four, the sum
of squares is one hundred and six,

96
00:04:04,969 --> 00:04:06,689
and there are six numbers.

97
00:04:06,689 --> 00:04:08,075
The mean stays four.

98
00:04:08,575 --> 00:04:12,964
The new variance is one hundred
and six over six, minus sixteen.

99
00:04:12,964 --> 00:04:14,763
That is five over three.

100
00:04:15,363 --> 00:04:16,904
Here is your recap.

101
00:04:16,904 --> 00:04:18,488
Screenshot this card.

102
00:04:21,688 --> 00:04:23,874
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

103
00:04:24,274 --> 00:04:25,174
One.

104
00:04:24,563 --> 00:04:25,814
Why do we square the deviations?

105
00:04:26,714 --> 00:04:27,614
Two.

106
00:04:27,203 --> 00:04:31,192
If the variance is twenty five,
what is the standard deviation?

107
00:04:32,092 --> 00:04:32,992
Three.

108
00:04:32,552 --> 00:04:34,326
Heights are measured in centimetres.

109
00:04:34,326 --> 00:04:36,153
What is the unit of their variance?

110
00:04:37,253 --> 00:04:41,714
Next time: the weather report says
seventy percent chance of rain tomorrow.

111
00:04:41,714 --> 00:04:43,852
What does that number really mean?

112
00:04:43,852 --> 00:04:45,832
Probability, events and axioms.
